{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_14","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/sh -lc \"python - <<'PY'\nimport json\nfrom pathlib import Path\nclaims=json.loads(Path('claims_grounding.json').read_text())['claims']\nfor index,claim in enumerate(claims,1):\n print(index,claim['claim'])\n print('  citations:',claim.get('citation_keys',[]))\nprint('\\\\nSelected existing provenance abstracts:')\nrefs=json.loads(Path('references_provenance.json').read_text())['references']\nfor ref in refs:\n if ref['citation_key'] in ['Polo2024','Kipnis2025','Andersen1970','Liang2023','Wang2024','Elfving1952','Kiefer1960']:\n  print(ref['citation_key'],ref.get('abstract'))\ndata=json.loads(Path('paper_data.json').read_text())\nfinal=data['final_evaluation']\nprint('\\\\nFinal state:',final['status'],final['outcome'],final['correct'])\nprint('Final limitations:',final['limitations'])\nprint('Episode structure:',list(final['evaluation_evidence']))\nprint('First solution keys:',list(final['evaluation_evidence']['episodes'][0]['solution']))\nPY\"","aggregated_output":"1 The final evaluation completed but did not qualify; no conditional reaggregation changes this status.\n  citations: []\n2 The final evidence covers ten fixed benchmark cases, four Leaderboard-derived and six HELM-derived, with seeds 5003, 7001 and 9001 and budgets 8, 16 and 32.\n  citations: []\n3 Primary and all reference predictions are absent for Commonsense at seeds 5003 and 9001. Constant, copy, permuted and selected-item-mean structural checks fail there, while fitted uniform selection is available. The underlying exception is not identified by these records.\n  citations: []\n4 Common-support comparisons use 28 of 30 case-seed pairs, with Commonsense supported only at seed 7001. All other cases have three supported seeds.\n  citations: []\n5 Reported primary aggregate MAE is 0.08801465854359276 and RMSE 0.09789336720441476, including missing-output defaults.\n  citations: []\n6 Reported primary Spearman is 0.7328732236748521 and Kendall tau-b 0.5943160224743321; these include missing-record defaults.\n  citations: ['Spearman1904', 'Kendall1938']\n7 Reported fitted-uniform MAE 0.044910877034389785, selected-mean MAE 0.10836174574059959, uniform-mean MAE 0.1335974367939037, IRT MAE 0.1186668212839868, anchor MAE 0.22047382735923254, and source-mean MAE 0.1643825693696333 have different missing-output exposure and do not form a measured-only superiority ranking.\n  citations: []\n8 Structural-control failure summary is 0.041666666666666664, missing-evaluation fraction is 0.011049723756906077, and checked replay maximum discrepancy is zero. The first two are evaluator-defined summaries, not rates over independent target models or case-seed pairs.\n  citations: []\n9 The stored primary-minus-anchor contrast is -0.0769036132600842 with endpoints [-1,1]; these are fallback endpoints because pairing is incomplete, not an informative confidence interval. Its missing-record convention differs from separately penalized marginal MAEs.\n  citations: ['Efron1979']\n10 Available-pair primary MAE is lower than fitted-uniform MAE in nine cases; MedQA is the exception at 0.0574 versus 0.0530. This is descriptive and includes singleton-support Commonsense.\n  citations: []\n11 Conditional BBH primary and fitted-uniform MAEs are 0.0308 and 0.0570, and MATH MAEs are 0.0603 and 0.0624. MuSR anchor error is heterogeneous relative to the other cases.\n  citations: []\n12 Under equal-source/equal-case and within-case available-seed normalization, primary MAEs at budgets 8/16/32 are 0.0438507255604756, 0.03333117402076665 and 0.027776433747489636.\n  citations: []\n13 On identical common support, fitted-uniform MAEs at budgets 8/16/32 are 0.05983060181043318, 0.04388863282142454 and 0.03283671741622329.\n  citations: []\n14 Selected-item mean MAEs at budgets 8/16/32 are 0.13207754853140033, 0.10495817584576625 and 0.08507833899220836; uniform-mean MAEs are 0.10821494499086409, 0.08229789663800609 and 0.05556974653061864. Uniform raw means have lower MAE at every displayed budget, whereas fitted selection reverses the conditional ordering.\n  citations: []\n15 Conditional primary Spearman correlations at budgets 8/16/32 are 0.7787133623969075, 0.8485275353348883 and 0.8858252382784845. Selected-mean correlations are 0.7805746798716318, 0.8461155247695583 and 0.8818752839767907. Reconstruction changes absolute-score error much more than ranking in this retained evidence, without isolating a particular reconstruction component.\n  citations: []\n16 All numerical cells of supplementary case and budget tables and both quantitative figures are deterministic transformations of recorded final target means and predictions on their declared common support. Seed dots are descriptive, with overlapping model populations, and are not confidence intervals.\n  citations: []\n17 The supplementary seed table retains every primary case-seed record, recorded target/group counts, and budget-averaged MAE/RMSE/Spearman/Kendall. Missing rows remain missing. The exhaustive CSV separates each budget, condition and reference with availability/structural status, using the evaluator's zero rank-correlation convention for exactly constant vectors.\n  citations: []\n18 The remaining reported diagnostic MAEs are 0.22204546910278564 for constant observations, 0.15448451324975213 for copied observations, 0.1005045540030643 for permuted observations and 0.02807015578570595 for the single checked replay; they are not ordinary independent target-accuracy estimates.\n  citations: []\n19 The evaluated source-only selector uses equal total group weights, empirical score covariance, diagonal nugget 0.1 max(mu(1-mu),0.05), and greedy squared conditional covariance divided by conditional item variance floored at 1e-12. It uses low-rank updates and no target-adaptive acquisition.\n  citations: ['Elfving1952', 'Kiefer1960']\n20 The linear branch fits row-unweighted centered ridge regression with an intercept and shrinkage. Per-budget strength grid is 0.1/1/10 and shrinkage grid 0/0.5/1, selected by group-weighted source-held-out squared error.\n  citations: ['Hoerl1970']\n21 The source profile branch fixes smoothed-logit abilities, initializes difficulties from clipped group-weighted item means, and uses eight bounded Newton updates with difficulty penalty 0.01. It is not joint or conditional maximum-likelihood Rasch estimation.\n  citations: ['Andersen1970']\n22 Profiles interpolate Rasch probabilities with binary source responses and clip at 1e-6. Each source contributes five ability-offset particles with offsets h*(-2,-1,0,1,2) and prior proportions (1,4,6,4,1)/16; total mass is equal per source group.\n  citations: []\n23 Posterior normalization uses log-sum-exp and adds the exact observed total to the expected unqueried total. Every prefix recomputes from the original prior; the posterior is not a calibrated empirical uncertainty interval.\n  citations: []\n24 At most three group-disjoint source folds choose residual weight 0/0.5/0.75, offset scale 0/0.5 and blend 0/0.5/1, by group-balanced MAE across budgets, with simpler tie-breaking. One-group fallback is ridge strength one, shrinkage one and zero profile blend. No final fitted blend logs or independent final component ablations are available.\n  citations: ['Varma2006', 'Arlot2010', 'Stone1974']\n25 The uniform fitted control changes acquisition and refits/tunes reconstruction. The selected-mean control returns the raw mean on the primary order. The uniform mean uses the same prescribed seeded uniform order as the fitted uniform control. These compare configured procedures or whole reconstruction, not isolated particle or selector effects.\n  citations: []\n26 The evaluator uses seed-hashed approximately 70/30 model-group splits and equal source-domain, within-domain case, seed and budget weights. Metadata grouping is conservative and cannot prove full training-lineage separation.\n  citations: ['Roberts2017', 'BenDavid2010']\n27 The anchor reference clusters centered normalized response vectors using one initialization and at most 30 iterations; scores are representative responses weighted by nearest-representative assignments. This is a local reference, not an exact published reproduction.\n  citations: ['Vivek2024']\n28 The IRT reference uses a penalized 2PL model, L-BFGS-B with at most 40 iterations and information-ranked fixed items, followed by up to 30 regularized Newton ability updates. It averages full-universe model probabilities and is not an exact tinyBenchmarks reproduction.\n  citations: ['Polo2024']\n29 Identical queried response rows receive the same estimator output without target identity/group inputs. The prevalence of indistinguishable target pairs is not measured; this is a source-inspectable interface property, not a new theorem or empirical finding.\n  citations: []\n30 Prior permitted work covers representative anchors, efficient benchmark score estimation, sparse ability-based benchmarks, reference-instance assessors and learned evaluation acquisition. These citations provide context, not performance measurements for this study.\n  citations: ['Vivek2024', 'Polo2024', 'Kipnis2025', 'Pacchiardi2024', 'Li2025']\n\nSelected existing provenance abstracts:\nPolo2024 The versatility of large language models (LLMs) led to the creation of diverse benchmarks that thoroughly test a variety of language models' abilities. These benchmarks consist of tens of thousands of examples making evaluation of LLMs very expensive. In this paper, we investigate strategies to reduce the number of evaluations needed to assess the performance of an LLM on several key benchmarks. For example, we show that to accurately estimate the performance of an LLM on MMLU, a popular multiple-choice QA benchmark consisting of 14K examples, it is sufficient to evaluate this LLM on 100 curated examples. We release evaluation tools and tiny versions of popular benchmarks: Open LLM Leaderboard, MMLU, HELM, and AlpacaEval 2.0. Our empirical analysis demonstrates that these tools and tiny benchmarks are sufficient to reliably and efficiently reproduce the original evaluation results.\nKipnis2025 Large Language Models (LLMs) vary in their abilities on a range of tasks. Initiatives such as the Open LLM Leaderboard aim to quantify these differences with several large benchmarks (sets of test items to which an LLM can respond either correctly or incorrectly). However, high correlations within and between benchmark scores suggest that (1) there exists a small set of common underlying abilities that these benchmarks measure, and (2) items tap into redundant information and the benchmarks may thus be considerably compressed. We use data from n > 5000 LLMs to identify the most informative items of six benchmarks, ARC, GSM8K, HellaSwag, MMLU, TruthfulQA and WinoGrande (with d = 28,632 items in total). From them we distill a sparse benchmark, metabench, that has less than 3% of the original size of all six benchmarks combined. This new sparse benchmark goes beyond point scores by yielding estimators of the underlying benchmark-specific abilities. We show that these estimators (1) can be used to reconstruct each original individual benchmark score with, on average, 1.24% root mean square error (RMSE), (2) reconstruct the original total score with 0.58% RMSE, and (3) have a single underlying common factor whose Spearman correlation with the total score is r = 0.94.\nLiang2023 Language models (LMs) are becoming the foundation for almost all major language technologies, but their capabilities, limitations, and risks are not well understood. We present Holistic Evaluation of Language Models (HELM) to improve the transparency of language models. First, we taxonomize the vast space of potential scenarios (i.e. use cases) and metrics (i.e. desiderata) that are of interest for LMs. Then we select a broad subset based on coverage and feasibility, noting what's missing or underrepresented (e.g. question answering for neglected English dialects, metrics for trustworthiness). Second, we adopt a multi-metric approach: We measure 7 metrics (accuracy, calibration, robustness, fairness, bias, toxicity, and efficiency) for each of 16 core scenarios when possible (87.5% of the time). This ensures metrics beyond accuracy don't fall to the wayside, and that trade-offs are clearly exposed. We also perform 7 targeted evaluations, based on 26 targeted scenarios, to analyze specific aspects (e.g. reasoning, disinformation). Third, we conduct a large-scale evaluation of 30 prominent language models (spanning open, limited-access, and closed models) on all 42 scenarios, 21 of which were not previously used in mainstream LM evaluation. Prior to HELM, models on average were evaluated on just 17.9% of the core HELM scenarios, with some prominent models not sharing a single scenario in common. We improve this to 96.0%: now all 30 models have been densely benchmarked on the same core scenarios and metrics under standardized conditions. Our evaluation surfaces 25 top-level findings. For full transparency, we release all raw model prompts and completions publicly for further analysis, as well as a general modular toolkit. We intend for HELM to be a living benchmark for the community, continuously updated with new scenarios, metrics, and models.\nWang2024 In the age of large-scale language models, benchmarks like the Massive Multitask Language Understanding (MMLU) have been pivotal in pushing the boundaries of what AI can achieve in language comprehension and reasoning across diverse domains. However, as models continue to improve, their performance on these benchmarks has begun to plateau, making it increasingly difficult to discern differences in model capabilities. This paper introduces MMLU-Pro, an enhanced dataset designed to extend the mostly knowledge-driven MMLU benchmark by integrating more challenging, reasoning-focused questions and expanding the choice set from four to ten options. Additionally, MMLU-Pro eliminates the trivial and noisy questions in MMLU. Our experimental results show that MMLU-Pro not only raises the challenge, causing a significant drop in accuracy by 16% to 33% compared to MMLU but also demonstrates greater stability under varying prompts. With 24 different prompt styles tested, the sensitivity of model scores to prompt variations decreased from 4-5% in MMLU to just 2% in MMLU-Pro. Additionally, we found that models utilizing Chain of Thought (CoT) reasoning achieved better performance on MMLU-Pro compared to direct answering, which is in stark contrast to the findings on the original MMLU, indicating that MMLU-Pro includes more complex reasoning questions. Our assessments confirm that MMLU-Pro is a more discriminative benchmark to better track progress in the field.\nElfving1952 If for the estimation of $\\beta_1, \\beta_2$ different observations (``sources'') of form (1.1) are potentially available, each of them being repeatable as many times as we please, the question arises which of them the experimenter should utilize, and in what proportions. With appropriate optimality conventions the answer is the following. For the estimation of a single quantity of form $\\theta = \\alpha_1\\beta_1 + \\alpha_2\\beta_2$ the optimum allocation comprises two sources only; for the estimation of both parameters, the corresponding number is two or three; the best proportions are indicated in Sections 2 and 4 below. Generalizations to more than two parameters and to observations at different costs are briefly discussed. The problem is related to Hotelling's weighing problem [2] and to the topics treated by David and Neyman in [1].\nKiefer1960 Let f1 , …, fk be linearly independent real functions on a space X, such that the range R of (f1, …, fk) is a compact set in k dimensional Euclidean space. (This will happen, for example, if the fi are continuous and X is a compact topological space.) Let S be any Borel field of subsets of X which includes X and all sets which consist of a finite number of points, and let C = {ε} be any class of probability measures on S which includes all probability measures with finite support (that is, which assign probability one to a set consisting of a finite number of points), and which are such that is defined. In all that follows we consider only probability measures ε which are in C.\nAndersen1970 Summary The problem of obtaining consistent estimates for structural parameters in the presence of infinitely many incidental parameters was discussed first by Neyman and Scott (1948). In this paper a maximum-likelihood method based on the conditional distribution given minimal sufficient statistics for the incidental parameters is suggested. It is proved that conditional maximum-likelihood estimates in the regular case are consistent and asymptotically normally distributed with a simple asymptotic variance. The efficiency problem of this new estimator is discussed in particular with respect to some situations with ancillary information.\n\nFinal state: completed not_qualified False\nFinal limitations: ['Final evaluation completed but did not qualify; search success does not establish final qualification.', 'Non-qualification may reflect performance, uncertainty, validity, adaptivity, or other criteria; report only causes supported by evaluator evidence.', 'Retained metrics do not independently establish claims whose qualification or validity checks failed. Missing gate diagnostics remain unknown.']\nEpisode structure: ['candidate_diagnostics_trust', 'episode_count', 'episodes', 'path', 'sha256', 'status']\nFirst solution keys: ['budgets', 'cases', 'fixed_item_universes']\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
